+ Washington’s Southern Tour – Horry County Historical Society Skip to main content Search Close Search search Menu HOME ABOUT COLLECTIONS MISC. ITEMS MEETINGS/EVENTS MEMBERSHIP DONATE AFFILIATES search Washington’s Southern Tour On April 27, 1791, President George Washington spent the night nearby at the indigo plantation of Jeremiah Vereen. He wrote in his diary that he was “entertained (& very kindly) without being able to make compensation.” The next day Vereen guided Washington across Lewis Swash (now Singleton Swash) and onto the strand at Long Bay (now Myrtle Beach). Erected by Horry County, 2008 replacing a marker erected by the Horry County Historical Society in 1941 King’s Rode, (SC Sec. Rd. 26-559), Myrtle Beach, SC GPS: 33° 46.722′ N, 78° 46.336′ W Washington’s Southern Tour (1791) historical marker A portion of the original Broad Road which George Washington traveled in 1791. Photo taken in the 1930s. Share this: Share on Facebook (Opens in new window) Facebook Share on X (Opens in new window) X Site Use Permission to use text or images from the Horry County Historical Society website is required for any purpose beyond personal use. Some uses requiring permission are: publication, display, advertising, or posting elsewhere on the Internet. This website is owned by the Horry County Historical Society, 606 Main Street, Conway, SC 29526-4340. A 501(c)(3) non-profit organization Report errors in historical content to the HCHS via the “Contact Us” form on this page. Contact Us Name * First Last Email * Name Email or Comment or Message * Submit © 2026 Horry County Historical Society. All Rights Reserved. facebook email Close Menu HOME ABOUT COLLECTIONS MISC. ITEMS MEETINGS/EVENTS MEMBERSHIP DONATE AFFILIATES 
